  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Package Installation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on determining the necessary packages for this project, particularly `pdf-lib`. Considering the user\'s request for a CDN-based import and our use of React, Vite, and TypeScript, I\'ve concluded a direct installation of `pdf-lib` via npm is the most efficient and straightforward approach. I\'m exploring the implications for bundling and potential build-time optimizations.\n\n\n**Confirming Package Installation**\n\nI\'ve decided to proceed with installing `pdf-lib` via npm using our `install_applet_package` tool, the most effective choice for our React/Vite/TypeScript setup. I\'ve confirmed that the package name on npm is `pdf-lib`, and installation should be straightforward. I\'m now contemplating the best way to handle sticker PDFs, especially considering that they might not exist on disk, I\'m considering options for uploading PDFs or creating them programmatically.\n\n\n**Developing Dynamic Sticker Generation**\n\nI\'m now expanding on the sticker PDF generation. I\'ve realized we can *dynamically build* round stickers as PDF bytes using `pdf-lib` when generating the A4 sheet. This will make it far more functional and independent from server-side files. I can draw circles, add icons and text on-the-fly, creating a library of mock stickers! It\'s an impressive improvement over the initial approach.\n\n\n**Implementing PDF Embedding**\n\nI\'ve realized the power of `pdf-lib` for *directly embedding* user-uploaded PDF sticker pages onto the A4 grid. Using `embedPages` and similar methods, I can now meet their exact request \342\200\223 they upload a single-page PDF sticker, and the app seamlessly places it on the A4. This streamlines sticker creation and provides a perfect solution. I am now planning to bundle default stickers, both visual representations and dynamically generated PDFs, and an upload feature.\n\n\n**Refining PDF Embedding**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the PDF embedding process with `pdf-lib`. A user\'s uploaded single-page sticker PDF should seamlessly merge onto the A4 grid. I\'ll load the PDF and embed its page into the A4 document, applying a round sticker shape, perfectly fulfilling the user\'s requirement. I\'ve re-examined the API and it looks straightforward. I can ensure the output is a real PDF with a round sticker inside!\n\n\n**Refining Sticker Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how we handle round stickers. I realized that if the sticker PDF is already round, embedding it as-is is the *correct* way to proceed, perfectly simulating professional printing. Square PDFs can be applied directly! No masking is required in this case, meaning greater efficiency. I\'ll need to confirm sticker dimensions and placement on the A4.\n\n\n**Designing Sticker Sheet Layouts**\n\nI\'m now determining how best to arrange the sticker sheet layout. I think it is important to include preset layouts like 3x4, 4x6, and 5x8 grids for standard sticker sizes to streamline the experience. I will also incorporate customizable grids so that the user can get a layout that matches their needs. This lets them select the grid, which creates an A4 PDF, then populates the stickers.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 13
      nanos: 527530993
    }
