      nanos: 821345684
    }
  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Application**\n\nI\'m now focusing on application design. My primary goal is a beautiful and highly functional sticker selection and merging tool. The key question I\'m tackling is how to best simulate or merge sticker PDFs, given the absence of actual files like `sticker1_bedankt.pdf` in the project\'s public folder.\n\n\n**Rendering Dynamic Stickers**\n\nI\'ve shifted focus to dynamic sticker rendering within the downloadable A4 PDF. The goal is a visually appealing and immediately usable sheet. I am considering user uploads of custom sticker PDFs, which I\'ll parse with `pdf-lib`. These uploads will be stored in-memory, labeled, and accompanied by dynamic thumbnails. Users will add stickers from \"De Bibliotheek\" (including preloaded designs) to \"De Selectie,\" which has controls for quantity, deletion, layout, margin presets, and clearing. I am also planning an interactive something something.\n\n\n**Implementing Live A4 Preview**\n\nI\'m now implementing a live A4 preview using Tailwind. This interactive preview, ideally placed in the middle or bottom, shows users precisely how the generated A4 sheet will look in real time. When generating the PDF, I will read the sticker list and their counts. Then I\'ll build a flat list of items to set up an A4 PDF document, calculate margins and cell dimensions based on the selected layout. Finally, I will calculate sticker diameter to render in each grid slot.\n\n\n**Drawing Dynamic Stickers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on dynamically drawing beautiful stickers. If it\'s a custom PDF, I embed the first page and scale it. For default stickers, I\'m drawing them with `pdf-lib`: filled circles, dotted borders, and text with standard PDF fonts. I can also add shapes like stars and hearts using drawing functions, and I\'m checking if I can import standard fonts. Finally, I will save the PDF as bytes and trigger a download.\n\n\n**Confirming PDF Color Handling**\n\nI\'ve verified `pdf-lib`\'s color handling. It uses `rgb(r, g, b)`, with values from 0 to 1, as expected. I\'ve also confirmed that I can convert hex codes with a simple utility: that\'s extremely clean!\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface layout. I\'m focusing on a clean design with a header, left section for sticker selection, and a central A4 preview.\n\n\n**Visualizing Sticker Library**\n\nI\'m now focusing on designing the \"De Bibliotheek\" column. I will show preloaded stickers from `stickers.json` as beautifully colored cards. Each card will display a Lucide icon, the sticker\'s name, and its text content. I\'m also planning an intuitive \"Sticker Toevoegen\" section and drag-and-drop area for user-uploaded sticker PDFs. I will parse uploaded PDFs, show previews, prompt for labels, and add them to the library for multiple selections. I want to make an interactive A4 live mockup, a visual \"De Bibliotheek\".\n\n\n**Refining Sticker Selection**\n\nI\'m now refining \"De Selectie\" column with selected stickers shown as thumbnails, titles, and a counter. I\'ll offer grid type dropdowns for different sizes, along with controls for spacing and margins. Clear selection and PDF generation are planned. A key addition is \"Fill entire sheet\" to simplify printing multiple stickers!\n\n\n\n**Organizing Code Structure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on organizing the codebase for modularity, as instructed. I\'m creating dedicated files: `/src/types.ts` for types, `/src/utils/pdfGenerator.ts` for PDF rendering with `pdf-lib`, `/src/components/Library.tsx` for the sticker library, and `/src/components/PreviewSheet.tsx` for the A4 preview. My goal is to ensure a maintainable and scalable structure, in line with the instructions. I have confirmed that the specified icons cover the application\'s needs.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 16
      nanos: 188907199
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
